.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.backup
*.tfplan
*.snap

# Generated by terraform/<cloud>/bootstrap, one per account or
# subscription. Not a secret — a bucket name and a region — but not a
# fact about this repository either, and a committed one is a bucket
# somebody else's fork tries to write to. The .example files beside them
# are committed and explain each key.
terraform/*/backend.hcl

# .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored — it is committed. It
# pins provider versions and records their checksums, so CI can't be
# broken by an upstream release and a substituted provider can't install
# silently. Regenerate with `terraform providers lock` (see
# docs/deployment.md), never by hand.
#
# Nothing below is attached to that note; it describes an absence.

.DS_Store

# Vault rotation script state (accessors only, not secrets — but keep out of the repo)
.vault-rotation-state/

# Local development TLS material, generated by scripts/generate-dev-certs.sh.
# Private keys must never be committed — a key in version control is
# compromised from the moment it lands, whether or not anyone notices.
docker/dev/tls/

# Recovery keys from bootstrap-dev-cluster.sh. With a seal stanza,
# `operator init` returns these instead of unseal keys, and they are what
# `generate-root` and `rekey` need. Dev-only and regenerated on every
# bootstrap — but a key is a key, and none of them belong in git.
# Every variant, not just the exact name. rotate-keys.sh writes
# .recovery-keys.json.new before verification and leaves
# .recovery-keys.json.superseded after it, and both hold shares that
# were live minutes ago.
docker/dev/.recovery-keys.json*

# Vault Agent credentials, written by scripts/bootstrap-agent.sh.
# role_id is not a secret; secret_id is, and Agent deletes it after
# reading it anyway. Neither belongs in the repository.
docker/dev/agent/

# Per-machine Claude Code settings. `.local` is the convention for
# "this developer's copy": it records which tool invocations they have
# approved, which is machine state rather than anything the project
# needs, and it grows as they work.
#
# Ignored by name rather than ignoring .claude/ wholesale, so a shared
# settings.json or a checked-in skill can still be committed on purpose.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Spec Kit scaffolding, installed into this checkout by the Specify CLI
# rather than by anything here. No tracked file in this repository refers
# to `.specify` or to these skills, no CI job runs them, and the Makefile
# does not know about them — so they are one developer's tooling sitting
# in a shared tree, and they made `git status` noisy on every branch.
#
# Named rather than wholesale, following the line above: if this project
# ever adopts Spec Kit, committing `.specify/` and its skills becomes a
# deliberate act with a commit message, not something that happens because
# an ignore rule stopped covering them.
.specify/
.claude/skills/speckit-*/
